★ [acc-beliefs-097]
  "Asymmetric Remediation Velocity" — A Belief within accelerationist discourse that AI vulnerability remediation often requires compute-intensive model retraining that fundamentally outpaces standardized reporting and patching intervals.
Encompasses: physical compute constraints on model updates, latency between flaw identification and retraining deployment, the structural mismatch between software patching and model weight adjustment.
  Vulnerability: The emergence of parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) and adapter-based architectures effectively decouples minor vulnerability remediation from full-scale retraining, rendering the perceived 'asymmetry' a temporary artifact of current training paradigms rather than a structural necessity.
★ [acc-beliefs-028] (confidence: 0.72)
  "Asymmetric Compliance Overhead on Open-Source Software Under Upstream Liability" — A Belief within accelerationist discourse that upstream compliance mandates and criminal penalties impose asymmetric fixed costs that harm decentralized open-source development.
Encompasses: regulatory compliance asymmetry across developer scales, legal overhead for non-commercial maintainers, anti-competitive market consolidation through upstream gatekeeping, safe harbor necessity for public repositories, physical infrastructure permitting friction.
  Vulnerability: While compliance creates friction, well-designed safe harbors and tiered liability thresholds can exempt non-commercial upstream developers while ensuring accountability for commercial actors, meaning the asymmetry is a policy implementation flaw rather than an inherent feature of upstream regulation.
★ [acc-beliefs-094] (confidence: 0.73)
  "Asymmetry Between Reconstructive Burden and Remediation Cost" — A Belief within accelerationist discourse that AI systems possess lower overhead for error correction compared to human cognitive processes.
Encompasses: Algorithmic auditability, post-hoc reconstruction, error isolation, scalable remediation, technical transparency.
  Vulnerability: The claim ignores the 'semantic drift' and 'alignment tax' associated with localizing and patching specific behaviors in high-dimensional latent spaces, which may create emergent, harder-to-detect errors elsewhere in the model.
