- [acc-intentions-078] Create Regulatory Conditions Where Market Competition Drives AI Transparency: Optimism bias — The proposal assumes competitive pressure will reliably drive firms toward open-sourcing security-critical capabilities, overestimating the market's intrinsic tendency toward transparency when proprietary advantage is commercially dominant.
- [acc-intentions-099] Integrating Mandatory Pre-Deployment Auditing with Strict Liability Enforcement: false dilemma — The node frames the choice as binary—either pre-deployment safety gates or post-deployment monitoring with strict liability—when in practice most robust governance regimes combine both, and treating them as mutually exclusive is a structural misrepresentation.
- [acc-intentions-115] Deploying Dynamic Machine-Speed Defensive AI Over Static Computational Pauses: False Dilemma — The node frames the choice as between rapid offensive-pace defensive AI development and either vulnerability or harmful restriction, excluding the possibility that hybrid strategies combining technical development with international agreements could yield better security outcomes.
- [acc-intentions-115] Deploying Dynamic Machine-Speed Defensive AI Over Static Computational Pauses: Optimism Bias — The strategy assumes defenders can consistently maintain a technological edge in a domain where offense historically holds structural advantages (lower cost, asymmetric access, attribution difficulty), underestimating the plausibility of a race that defenders systematically lose.
- [acc-beliefs-078] Targeted Real-Time Safety Investment Is More Efficient Than Front-Loading: False Precision — The claim anchors on a specific 5-7% investment range as 'optimal,' presenting a quantified figure without empirical grounding for why that band is universally appropriate across heterogeneous AI systems and risk profiles.
- [acc-beliefs-078] Targeted Real-Time Safety Investment Is More Efficient Than Front-Loading: Hasty Generalization — The argument draws on agile development practices — effective for iterative software — and generalizes to AI safety contexts where failure modes can be irreversible, ignoring the domain-specific limits of that analogy.
- [acc-desires-039] Democratize AI Through Open-Source Proliferation to Prevent Oligarchic Control: False Dilemma — The node frames the choice as binary — open-source democratization versus oligarchic consolidation — obscuring a rich space of hybrid governance models (e.g., tiered access, regulated open licensing, public compute utilities with access controls) that could address both concerns.
- [acc-desires-039] Democratize AI Through Open-Source Proliferation to Prevent Oligarchic Control: Optimism Bias — Assumes that broad distribution of cognitive utility will naturally produce equitable outcomes while systematically underweighting the potential for malicious exploitation, regulatory capture of antitrust mechanisms, or the reproduction of existing power asymmetries within open ecosystems.
- [acc-beliefs-070] Output-Based Verification in Ground-Truth Independent Domains: False Dichotomy — The claim frames the choice as binary—internal inspection vs. output-based verification—ignoring hybrid approaches that combine mechanistic interpretability research with behavioral testing.
- [acc-intentions-105] Replace Centralized Safety Audits with Trustless Cryptographic Verification: Nirvana Fallacy — The proposal implicitly compares an idealized trustless system against the flaws of real centralized audits, while ignoring the practical failure modes (low adoption, oracle problems, incomplete specifications) of decentralized cryptographic verification in production AI contexts.

